# Python Adapters, Models, GUI

## Layers

```text
gui/ / cli/     → user-facing only; call models or adapters
models/         → orchestration, ML SDKs, business flow
adapters/       → domain facades (Ai, Dubbing, FFmpeg, Media, …)
adapters/rust_bridge/ → binary IPC only (adapters may import; models/gui must not)
```

## Domain adapters (prefer these)

| Adapter | Import | Owns |
|:---|:---|:---|
| AI | `from claynext.adapters.ai import get_ai_adapter` | LLM retry/keys, OCR filter, `scan_burned_captions`, expression tags, VAD, subtitle chunk |
| Dubbing | `from claynext.adapters.dubbing import get_dubbing_adapter` | Speech quota, dub prompt, align speed, duck volume |
| FFmpeg | `from claynext.adapters.ffmpeg_adapter import get_ffmpeg_adapter` | Probe, duration, stills, PCM decode |
| Media / Codecs / Engine | `get_media_adapter`, `get_hardware_codecs`, `get_rust_engine` | Proxy, waveforms, GPU encode, timeline engine |

## Migration mindset

- Old: all logic in Python models/services.
- New: **Rust owns core computation**; Python owns GUI, I/O SDKs, and orchestration.
- When touching a hot path (quota, prompt, tempo, VAD, probe), check `backends/` / `crates/` first and extend the adapter instead of growing Python-only code.

## Fallbacks

```python
result = get_dubbing_adapter().estimate_speech_quota(dur, lang)
# adapter already falls back if claynext-ai missing
```

Never crash the GUI if a Rust binary is absent — log and degrade gracefully.
